export const THEORY_CONTENT={"level-0-notes":{title:"Learning the Notes on the Guitar Fretboard",concise:"\n<p>Music uses <strong>12 notes</strong>: A, A#/B♭, B, C, C#/D♭, D, D#/E♭, E, F, F#/G♭, G, G#/A♭. After the 12th fret the pattern repeats one octave higher.</p>\n<p><strong>Open strings</strong> in standard tuning (low → high): <strong>E – A – D – G – B – E</strong>. Each fret raises the pitch by one half step. B→C and E→F are natural half steps — no sharp between them.</p>\n<h4>Two approaches to learn the fretboard</h4>\n<p><strong>Horizontal bands:</strong> Natural notes form three diagonal bands across the neck. The two E strings are identical (learn once, get two free). The D string repeats the same pattern shifted 2 frets right. C D E fill the gaps on A, G and B strings.</p>\n<p><strong>Vertical columns (Circle of Fifths):</strong> Guitar is tuned in perfect fourths — the same interval that connects notes in the Circle of Fifths. Open strings E A D G follow the <strong>BEADGCF</strong> sequence (Circle of Fifths in fourths direction). Anchor columns at frets 0, 5, 7 and 10 each give a slice of this sequence. The B string (string 2) is the one exception: tuned a major 3rd above G instead of a 4th.</p>\n<p>Use the <strong>Note Groups</strong> tab in the Explore step to see both approaches visualised on the fretboard.</p>\n",detailed:'\n<h3>The 12 Notes of Music</h3>\n<p>Western music divides the octave into <strong>12 equally spaced semitones</strong>. On guitar each fret equals one semitone:</p>\n<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.05em; font-weight:600;">\nA — A#/B♭ — B — C — C#/D♭ — D — D#/E♭ — E — F — F#/G♭ — G — G#/A♭\n</p>\n<p>There is <strong>no sharp/flat between B &amp; C</strong> and <strong>no sharp/flat between E &amp; F</strong>. These are the two natural half-step pairs.</p>\n\n<h3>Standard Tuning &amp; Open Strings</h3>\n<p>Guitar strings in standard tuning (thickest → thinnest): <strong>E A D G B E</strong>. Mnemonic: <em>Eddie Ate Dynamite, Good Bye Eddie</em>.</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>String</th><th>6 (low)</th><th>5</th><th>4</th><th>3</th><th>2</th><th>1 (high)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Note</td><td>E</td><td>A</td><td>D</td><td>G</td><td>B</td><td>E</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Each string is tuned a <strong>perfect 4th</strong> apart (5 semitones) — except G→B (string 3→2), which is a <strong>major 3rd</strong> (4 semitones). This single irregularity is the source of most fingering exceptions on guitar.</p>\n\n<h3>Approach 1 — Horizontal Bands</h3>\n<p>Natural notes cluster into diagonal bands that can be learned in three incremental steps:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>E strings (gray):</strong> The high e and low E strings are identical. Learn F G A B C D E on frets 1–12 once — you get two strings for free. This is your anchor.</li>\n  <li><strong>FGAB on D string (blue):</strong> The D string follows the same pattern as the E strings, shifted 2 frets to the right. F G A B appear at frets 3, 5, 7, 9. Only 4 new positions to learn.</li>\n  <li><strong>CDE on A, G, B strings (green):</strong> C D E fill the remaining gaps on the inner strings. The B string shifts one fret left compared to the others — a direct consequence of the G→B tuning irregularity.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These three bands cover all 7 natural notes across the entire fretboard. Sharps and flats always sit in the gaps between natural notes.</p>\n\n<h3>Approach 2 — Vertical Columns (Circle of Fifths)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Circle of Fifths</strong> orders all 12 notes by the interval of a fifth (C G D A E B F# / C F B♭ E♭ A♭ D♭ G♭). Reading it in the <em>opposite</em> direction gives the <strong>Circle of Fourths: F C G D A E B</strong> — or reversed, <strong>BEADGCF</strong>.</p>\n<p>This matters because guitar is tuned in perfect fourths. Going from string 6 to string 1, each open string is a fourth higher than the previous: E→A→D→G→(B)→E. The open strings E A D G are four consecutive steps of BEADGCF. This creates predictable vertical columns across the neck:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Fret 0 (open):</strong> E A D G on strings 6–3 — four consecutive BEADGCF notes. String 2 (B) breaks the chain because G→B is a major 3rd, not a 4th. C and F at fret 1 complete the sequence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fret 5:</strong> A D G C on strings 6–3 — the same pattern shifted up a perfect 4th. String 2 gives E instead of the expected F; F appears at fret 6.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fret 7:</strong> B E A D on strings 6–3 — another anchor column.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fret 10:</strong> D G C F on strings 6–3 — the final anchor. Together frets 0, 5, 7 and 10 cover all 7 natural notes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The B string (string 2) always sits one fret higher than the BEADGCF pattern predicts. This is the <em>only</em> exception. Once you internalise it, the entire fretboard follows a single rule.</p>\n\n<h3>Approach 3 — Octave Shapes</h3>\n<p>Octave shapes let you transfer a known note to a new position:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Same string, 12 frets up</strong> — identical note, one octave higher.</li>\n  <li><strong>Two strings toward treble, two frets up</strong> — works for string pairs 6→4, 5→3, 4→2.</li>\n  <li><strong>Two strings toward treble, three frets up</strong> — use when crossing the G→B boundary (string pairs 3→1).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Practice Sequence</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Note Map:</strong> Pick one note and find every instance on the fretboard. Start with E, A, D, G — the open string notes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Note Groups — Horizontal:</strong> Work through the three bands step by step. Each step reveals one new group of notes on the fretboard.</li>\n  <li><strong>Note Groups — Vertical:</strong> Work through the anchor columns at frets 0, 5, 7 and 10. See how the BEADGCF sequence from the Circle of Fifths appears across the strings.</li>\n  <li><strong>Note Groups — Combined:</strong> Both systems overlaid. The intersections of horizontal bands and vertical columns are the highest-priority positions to memorise.</li>\n  <li><strong>String Drill:</strong> Find all instances of a given note on one string at a time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Note Quiz:</strong> Random position — name the note instantly without thinking.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Why This Matters</h3>\n<p>Knowing note names is the <em>foundation</em> of all fretboard navigation. Without it, every chord shape, scale pattern and arpeggio is just a disconnected finger pattern. With note knowledge you can instantly transpose, communicate with other musicians, and understand <em>why</em> shapes work — not just <em>where</em> they are.</p>\n'},"level-1-intervals":{title:"Intervals on Guitar",concise:"\n<p>An <strong>interval</strong> is the distance between two pitches, measured in half steps (semitones). Intervals have a <strong>number</strong> (2nd, 3rd, …) and a <strong>quality</strong> (major, minor, perfect, augmented, diminished).</p>\n<h4>The 13 basic intervals</h4>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Name</th><th>Semitones</th><th>Example from C</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect Unison (P1)</td><td>0</td><td>C → C</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 2nd (m2)</td><td>1</td><td>C → D♭</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 2nd (M2)</td><td>2</td><td>C → D</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 3rd (m3)</td><td>3</td><td>C → E♭</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 3rd (M3)</td><td>4</td><td>C → E</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect 4th (P4)</td><td>5</td><td>C → F</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tritone (TT)</td><td>6</td><td>C → F#/G♭</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect 5th (P5)</td><td>7</td><td>C → G</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 6th (m6)</td><td>8</td><td>C → A♭</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 6th (M6)</td><td>9</td><td>C → A</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 7th (m7)</td><td>10</td><td>C → B♭</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 7th (M7)</td><td>11</td><td>C → B</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect Octave (P8)</td><td>12</td><td>C → C</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key insight:</strong> The major 3rd (4 semitones) vs minor 3rd (3 semitones) determines if a chord/scale is major or minor.</p>\n",detailed:'\n<h3>What Is an Interval?</h3>\n<p>An interval measures the <strong>distance between two pitches</strong>. On guitar, this translates directly to frets: each fret = 1 semitone. Understanding intervals lets you see the fretboard in terms of <em>relationships</em> rather than memorised note names.</p>\n\n<h3>Interval Qualities</h3>\n<p>Intervals are classified by <strong>quality</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Perfect</strong> intervals: Unison (P1), 4th (P4), 5th (P5), Octave (P8) — these are the most consonant.</li>\n  <li><strong>Major / Minor</strong> intervals: 2nds, 3rds, 6ths, 7ths — major is one semitone wider than minor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Augmented:</strong> one semitone wider than perfect or major.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diminished:</strong> one semitone narrower than perfect or minor.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Complete Semitone Reference</h3>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Semitones</th><th>Interval</th><th>From C</th><th>Sound Character</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>0</td><td>Perfect Unison</td><td>C</td><td>Same note</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1</td><td>Minor 2nd</td><td>D♭</td><td>Dissonant, tense</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>2</td><td>Major 2nd</td><td>D</td><td>Mild tension</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>3</td><td>Minor 3rd</td><td>E♭</td><td>Sad, dark</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>4</td><td>Major 3rd</td><td>E</td><td>Happy, bright</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>5</td><td>Perfect 4th</td><td>F</td><td>Open, strong</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>6</td><td>Tritone</td><td>F#/G♭</td><td>Unstable, "devil\'s interval"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>7</td><td>Perfect 5th</td><td>G</td><td>Power, stability (power chords)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>8</td><td>Minor 6th</td><td>A♭</td><td>Bittersweet</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>9</td><td>Major 6th</td><td>A</td><td>Warm, sweet</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>10</td><td>Minor 7th</td><td>B♭</td><td>Bluesy, wants resolution</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>11</td><td>Major 7th</td><td>B</td><td>Jazzy, dreamy</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>12</td><td>Octave</td><td>C</td><td>Same note, higher register</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Intervals on the Guitar Fretboard</h3>\n<p>On a <strong>single string</strong>, intervals are simply counting frets. Across strings, the shapes depend on the tuning offset:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Strings tuned a P4 apart (5 semitones): same fret on the next string = P4 up.</li>\n  <li>String 3→2 (G→B): tuned a M3 apart (4 semitones), so shapes shift by one fret.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Intervals in Scales</h3>\n<p>The <strong>major scale formula</strong> is defined by intervals from the root:</p>\n<p style="font-family:monospace;">R – M2 – M3 – P4 – P5 – M6 – M7</p>\n<p>The <strong>natural minor scale formula</strong>:</p>\n<p style="font-family:monospace;">R – M2 – m3 – P4 – P5 – m6 – m7</p>\n<p>The only differences are the <strong>3rd, 6th, and 7th</strong> — each is lowered by one semitone in the minor scale.</p>\n\n<h3>Intervals in Chords</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major chord</strong> = Root + Major 3rd + Perfect 5th (R – M3 – P5)</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor chord</strong> = Root + Minor 3rd + Perfect 5th (R – m3 – P5)</li>\n  <li><strong>Dominant 7th</strong> = R – M3 – P5 – m7</li>\n  <li><strong>Major 7th</strong> = R – M3 – P5 – M7</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor 7th</strong> = R – m3 – P5 – m7</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Why Intervals Matter</h3>\n<p>Intervals are the <strong>DNA of music theory</strong>. Every scale, chord, and arpeggio is defined by its interval structure. When you think in intervals instead of note names, patterns become <strong>movable</strong> — learn a shape once and use it in any key by simply moving the root.</p>\n'},"level-2-triads":{title:"Major & Minor Triads on Guitar",concise:"\n<p>A <strong>triad</strong> is a three-note chord built by stacking two intervals of a 3rd:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major triad</strong> = Root + Major 3rd (4 semitones) + Perfect 5th (7 semitones). The two internal intervals are M3 + m3.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor triad</strong> = Root + Minor 3rd (3 semitones) + Perfect 5th (7 semitones). The two internal intervals are m3 + M3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On guitar, triads are played across <strong>4 string groups</strong> (1-2-3, 2-3-4, 3-4-5, 4-5-6), each with <strong>3 inversions</strong> (root position, 1st inversion, 2nd inversion) — that's 12 shapes per chord quality to master.</p>\n<p><strong>Root position:</strong> Root is the lowest note. <strong>1st inversion:</strong> 3rd is the lowest. <strong>2nd inversion:</strong> 5th is the lowest.</p>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>What Are Triads?</h3>\n<p>Triads are the building blocks of harmony — three notes stacked in intervals of a third. They are the simplest complete chords and form the foundation of the entire chord system.</p>\n\n<h3>Building Major Triads</h3>\n<p>A major triad is built from the <strong>1st, 3rd, and 5th degrees</strong> of the major scale:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Root to 3rd = <strong>Major 3rd</strong> (4 semitones)</li>\n  <li>3rd to 5th = <strong>Minor 3rd</strong> (3 semitones)</li>\n  <li>Root to 5th = <strong>Perfect 5th</strong> (7 semitones)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: C major triad = C – E – G.</p>\n\n<h3>Building Minor Triads</h3>\n<p>A minor triad uses the <strong>1st, ♭3rd, and 5th degrees</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Root to ♭3rd = <strong>Minor 3rd</strong> (3 semitones)</li>\n  <li>♭3rd to 5th = <strong>Major 3rd</strong> (4 semitones)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: C minor triad = C – E♭ – G.</p>\n\n<h3>Inversions</h3>\n<p>Inversions rearrange which note is on the bottom:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Inversion</th><th>Lowest Note</th><th>Example (C major)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Root position</td><td>Root</td><td>C – E – G</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1st inversion</td><td>3rd</td><td>E – G – C</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>2nd inversion</td><td>5th</td><td>G – C – E</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Inversions let you play the same chord in different fretboard positions, creating smoother voice leading and more musical movement.</p>\n\n<h3>String Groups</h3>\n<p>On guitar, triads live on groups of 3 adjacent strings:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>SG1:</strong> Strings 1-2-3 (E-B-G)</li>\n  <li><strong>SG2:</strong> Strings 2-3-4 (B-G-D)</li>\n  <li><strong>SG3:</strong> Strings 3-4-5 (G-D-A)</li>\n  <li><strong>SG4:</strong> Strings 4-5-6 (D-A-E)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each string group × 3 inversions = <strong>12 unique shapes per triad quality</strong>. Note that shapes on SG1 differ slightly from other groups because of the B-string tuning offset.</p>\n\n<h3>CAGED Triad Shapes</h3>\n<p>The five CAGED chord forms (C, A, G, E, D) each contain triads within their structure. When you play a barre chord, you're playing an expanded triad. Learning to extract the triad from each CAGED shape helps you see the relationship between chords, arpeggios, and scales.</p>\n\n<h3>Using Triads Musically</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Rhythm playing:</strong> Triads give you compact voicings all over the neck — much more versatile than open chords stuck at the nut.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chord melody:</strong> Triads let you harmonise melody notes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Soloing:</strong> Outlining triads in your solos connects your lines to the harmony.</li>\n  <li><strong>Voice leading:</strong> Moving between inversions on the same string group creates smooth, connected chord movement.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"level-3a-caged":{title:"The CAGED System for Guitar",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>CAGED system</strong> uses the five basic open chord shapes — <strong>C, A, G, E, D</strong> — as movable templates that map the entire fretboard into five interconnected zones.</p>\n<p>Each shape can be played as a barre chord anywhere on the neck. The shapes connect in the order <strong>C → A → G → E → D → C</strong> (repeating), covering every fret. Each zone also defines a scale pattern and arpeggio pattern, tying chords, scales, and arpeggios together.</p>\n<p><strong>Root note patterns</strong> are the key — learn where the root falls in each shape and you can instantly find any chord anywhere.</p>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>What Is the CAGED System?</h3>\n<p>The guitar fretboard is a grid of notes that can feel overwhelming. The CAGED system simplifies it by using five familiar open chord shapes as <strong>movable frameworks</strong> that divide the neck into logical sections.</p>\n\n<h3>The Five Open Chord Shapes</h3>\n<p>The system is named after five open chords every beginner learns:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>C shape</strong> — root on string 5</li>\n  <li><strong>A shape</strong> — root on string 5 (the common A-form barre chord)</li>\n  <li><strong>G shape</strong> — root on string 6</li>\n  <li><strong>E shape</strong> — root on string 6 (the common E-form barre chord)</li>\n  <li><strong>D shape</strong> — root on string 4</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each of these shapes can be moved up the neck by barring across the fret where the nut would be.</p>\n\n<h3>How the Shapes Connect</h3>\n<p>The shapes connect in sequence: <strong>C → A → G → E → D → C → …</strong> The connecting point is always a shared root note between adjacent shapes. For example:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Open C chord (C shape) — root on 5th string, 3rd fret</li>\n  <li>That same root becomes the starting point for an A-shape barre chord</li>\n  <li>The A shape connects to the G shape via the root on the 6th string</li>\n  <li>G connects to E, E connects to D, and D wraps back to C</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>CAGED and Scales</h3>\n<p>Each CAGED chord shape has a corresponding <strong>major scale pattern</strong> that surrounds it. When you learn the 5 scale positions of the major scale, you're learning patterns that align with the 5 CAGED shapes. This creates a powerful connection:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The <strong>chord shape</strong> shows you the chord tones (root, 3rd, 5th)</li>\n  <li>The <strong>scale pattern</strong> shows you all 7 scale degrees around those chord tones</li>\n  <li>The <strong>arpeggio pattern</strong> highlights just the chord tones within the scale</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This means that in any position on the neck, you can instantly see <em>where the chord tones are</em> within the scale you're playing.</p>\n\n<h3>CAGED and Arpeggios</h3>\n<p>Each CAGED shape defines a major arpeggio pattern (Root, 3rd, 5th). Minor CAGED shapes give you minor arpeggios (Root, ♭3rd, 5th). These arpeggios sit inside the chord shape like a skeleton, giving you target notes for soloing.</p>\n\n<h3>Root Note Patterns</h3>\n<p>Each CAGED shape has a distinctive root note pattern — the arrangement of root notes across the strings. Learning these patterns lets you:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Instantly identify which CAGED zone you're in</li>\n  <li>Find the root note to orient yourself</li>\n  <li>Navigate between adjacent shapes smoothly</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Why CAGED Matters</h3>\n<p>CAGED is the <strong>organisational framework</strong> that ties everything together. Without it, scales, chords, and arpeggios exist as disconnected patterns. With CAGED, you see how they're all expressions of the same underlying structure, just viewed from different angles in different positions on the neck.</p>\n"},"level-4a-arpeggios":{title:"Major & Minor Arpeggios on Guitar",concise:'\n<p>An <strong>arpeggio</strong> is the notes of a chord played one at a time instead of strummed together. Think of it as a "chord-scale" containing only chord tones.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major arpeggio:</strong> Root – Major 3rd – Perfect 5th (intervals: R – M3 – P5)</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor arpeggio:</strong> Root – Minor 3rd – Perfect 5th (intervals: R – m3 – P5)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each arpeggio has <strong>5 CAGED shapes</strong> (C, A, G, E, D forms) that connect up the fretboard. Each shape can also be broken into practical <strong>3- and 4-note variations</strong> that are easier to incorporate into solos and fills.</p>\n<p>Arpeggios are essential for <strong>targeting chord tones</strong> — landing on the right notes when the chords change.</p>\n',detailed:"\n<h3>What Is an Arpeggio?</h3>\n<p>An arpeggio is when you take the notes of a chord and play them individually, one after the other, ascending or descending. You can think of an arpeggio as a scale made up of only chord tones.</p>\n\n<h3>Building Major Arpeggios</h3>\n<p>Major arpeggios are built from the notes of the major chord — the <strong>1st (root), 3rd, and 5th degrees</strong> of the major scale. In the major scale, isolate those three degrees to create a major arpeggio pattern.</p>\n\n<h3>Building Minor Arpeggios</h3>\n<p>Minor arpeggios use the <strong>root, ♭3rd, and 5th</strong> intervals of the minor scale. The minor arpeggio differs from the major arpeggio in that the 3rd interval is a minor 3rd (one semitone lower). This single note difference creates the major vs minor character.</p>\n\n<h3>CAGED Major Arpeggio Shapes</h3>\n<p>Each CAGED chord form (C, A, G, E, D) defines an arpeggio pattern across the fretboard:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>C shape:</strong> Derived from the C chord form. Lowest root on string 5. Includes the 3rd and 5th on the 6th string.</li>\n  <li><strong>A shape:</strong> From the A form chord. Lowest root on string 5. Includes the 5th on the 6th string and 3rd on the 4th string.</li>\n  <li><strong>G shape:</strong> From the G form barre chord. Lowest root on string 6. Includes the 5th on the 2nd string.</li>\n  <li><strong>E shape:</strong> From the E form barre chord. Lowest root on string 6. Includes the 3rd on the 5th string. Very commonly used.</li>\n  <li><strong>D shape:</strong> From the D form chord. Lowest root on string 4. Somewhat awkward to play in full form.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>CAGED Minor Arpeggio Shapes</h3>\n<p>The minor CAGED shapes follow the same principle but use the minor chord forms (Cm, Am, Gm, Em, Dm). The only difference is the ♭3rd replacing the natural 3rd.</p>\n\n<h3>3- and 4-Note Variations</h3>\n<p>Full arpeggio shapes span many frets and can be awkward. In practice, you'll use shorter fragments:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>3-note variations:</strong> Small fragments on adjacent strings — very practical for incorporating into licks.</li>\n  <li><strong>4-note variations:</strong> Slightly larger fragments that give more melodic material while remaining manageable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These smaller shapes are the most musically applicable and are what you'll actually use in solos and fills.</p>\n\n<h3>Arpeggio Technique</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Alternate picking:</strong> Alternating up/down strokes — the standard approach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sweep picking:</strong> A \"sweeping\" motion across strings — advanced technique, common in metal and neoclassical styles.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rolling technique:</strong> For notes side-by-side on the same fret, roll your finger to play them sequentially.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Why Learn Arpeggios?</h3>\n<p>Arpeggios give you a <strong>surgical tool</strong> for targeting chord tones in solos. While scales give you many note choices (some good, some risky), arpeggios contain <em>only</em> notes that will sound good over the current chord. They're a fail-safe way to outline chord changes and add harmonic colour to your playing.</p>\n"},"level-5a-pentatonic":{title:"Pentatonic Scales on Guitar",concise:'\n<p>The <strong>pentatonic scale</strong> is a 5-note scale — the most popular scale for guitar soloing. It removes the two "risky" notes from the 7-note major/minor scale, making almost every note sound good.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major pentatonic:</strong> R – M2 – M3 – P5 – M6 (drops the 4th and 7th)</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor pentatonic:</strong> R – m3 – P4 – P5 – m7 (drops the 2nd and ♭6th)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pentatonic has <strong>5 positions</strong> that connect up the neck (2 notes per string). Position 1 of the minor pentatonic is the most iconic shape in rock/blues guitar.</p>\n<p><strong>Key relationship:</strong> The major pentatonic of any key contains exactly the same notes as the minor pentatonic of its relative minor (e.g. G major pent = E minor pent).</p>\n',detailed:'\n<h3>Why the Pentatonic Scale Is So Popular</h3>\n<p>The pentatonic scale removes the two notes that create semitone (half-step) intervals in the major/minor scale. In the major scale, these are the 4th and 7th degrees. In the minor scale, they\'re the 2nd and ♭6th. These semitone intervals are considered <strong>dissonant</strong> — they create tension that needs resolution. Without them, the pentatonic scale contains only notes that will sound consonant over virtually any diatonic chord progression.</p>\n\n<h3>Major Pentatonic Scale</h3>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong> Root – Major 2nd – Major 3rd – Perfect 5th – Major 6th</p>\n<p>This scale has a bright, country/pop sound. It works beautifully over major chord progressions.</p>\n\n<h3>Minor Pentatonic Scale</h3>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong> Root – Minor 3rd – Perfect 4th – Perfect 5th – Minor 7th</p>\n<p>This scale has a dark, bluesy sound. It\'s the go-to scale for blues and rock soloing, and it can even be played over major chord progressions for a blues feel.</p>\n\n<h3>The 5 Pentatonic Positions</h3>\n<p>Like the major scale, the pentatonic breaks into 5 CAGED positions, each with a distinctive root note pattern:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Position 1:</strong> 3 root notes forming a triangle. The most commonly used position (the classic "blues box").</li>\n  <li><strong>Position 2:</strong> 2 root notes on strings 4 and 2. Contains position shifts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Position 3:</strong> 2 root notes on strings 5 and 2. Similar shape to position 5.</li>\n  <li><strong>Position 4:</strong> 2 root notes on strings 5 and 3. Very similar to position 1.</li>\n  <li><strong>Position 5:</strong> 3 root notes forming a triangle. The most "natural" feeling position.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Connecting the Shapes</h3>\n<p>Each position shares notes with the positions above and below it on the fretboard. After position 5, the patterns repeat starting with position 1. This means the entire fretboard is covered by five interlocking pentatonic shapes.</p>\n\n<h3>Movable Patterns</h3>\n<p>The scale patterns are <strong>not key-specific</strong>. To change keys, simply move the entire pattern so the root note aligns with your desired key. For example, moving G minor pentatonic up 2 frets gives you A minor pentatonic — same shapes, different root.</p>\n\n<h3>Major/Minor Pentatonic Relationship</h3>\n<p>The major pentatonic of any key uses the <em>exact same notes</em> as the minor pentatonic built from its relative minor. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>G major pentatonic: G A B D E</li>\n  <li>E minor pentatonic: E G A B D</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Same five notes, different tonal centre. This means learning one effectively teaches you both — you just shift which note you treat as "home."</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Learn all 5 positions in one key before moving to others.</li>\n  <li>Focus on root note patterns — they\'re your anchor points.</li>\n  <li>Practice connecting adjacent positions by finding shared notes.</li>\n  <li>Start and end phrases on root notes to reinforce the tonal centre.</li>\n</ul>\n'},"level-6a-diatonic":{title:"Major & Minor Scales: The Full Diatonic System",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>major scale</strong> follows the step pattern <strong>W–W–H–W–W–W–H</strong> (W = whole step, H = half step). The <strong>natural minor</strong> follows <strong>W–H–W–W–H–W–W</strong>, flattening the 3rd, 6th, and 7th.</p>\n<p>Each scale maps to <strong>5 CAGED positions</strong> covering the entire fretboard. The scale <em>extends</em> the pentatonic by adding two notes: the <strong>4th &amp; 7th</strong> (major) or <strong>2nd &amp; ♭6th</strong> (minor).</p>\n<p>Practice as: (1) single CAGED shapes ascending/descending, (2) harmonized through scale degrees, (3) over chord progressions in each CAGED variation.</p>\n",detailed:'\n<h3>The Major Scale</h3>\n<p>The major scale is the cornerstone of Western music. Its 7 notes follow a fixed step pattern:</p>\n<p><strong>Step pattern:</strong> Whole – Whole – Half – Whole – Whole – Whole – Half</p>\n<p>On guitar: a whole step = 2 frets, a half step = 1 fret.</p>\n\n<h4>Major Scale Intervals</h4>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Degree</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Root</td><td>Major 2nd</td><td>Major 3rd</td><td>Perfect 4th</td><td>Perfect 5th</td><td>Major 6th</td><td>Major 7th</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Semitones</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>7</td><td>9</td><td>11</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>The Natural Minor Scale</h3>\n<p>The natural minor scale has a darker, more emotional quality. It flattens the 3rd, 6th, and 7th degrees of the major scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Step pattern:</strong> Whole – Half – Whole – Whole – Half – Whole – Whole</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Degree</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>♭3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>♭6</th><th>♭7</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Root</td><td>Major 2nd</td><td>Minor 3rd</td><td>Perfect 4th</td><td>Perfect 5th</td><td>Minor 6th</td><td>Minor 7th</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Scale = Pentatonic + 2 Notes</h3>\n<p>The pentatonic scale is a <em>subset</em> of the full diatonic scale. Adding back the two "missing" notes gives you the complete scale:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major:</strong> Pentatonic (R, 2, 3, 5, 6) + <strong>4th</strong> and <strong>7th</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Minor:</strong> Pentatonic (R, ♭3, 4, 5, ♭7) + <strong>2nd</strong> and <strong>♭6th</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These added notes provide tension and resolution — the 7th pulls strongly toward the root (leading tone), and the 4th creates suspension against the 3rd.</p>\n\n<h3>5 Scale Positions (CAGED)</h3>\n<p>Both major and minor scales break into 5 positions following the CAGED system. Each position covers a 4–5 fret span with 2–3 notes per string:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>C form:</strong> Built around the open C chord shape</li>\n  <li><strong>A form:</strong> Built around the open A chord/barre chord shape</li>\n  <li><strong>G form:</strong> Built around the open G chord shape</li>\n  <li><strong>E form:</strong> Built around the E barre chord shape (most commonly taught first)</li>\n  <li><strong>D form:</strong> Built around the open D chord shape</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each position connects to the next via shared notes. After the D form, the patterns repeat starting with C.</p>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n<p>The three exercise types mirror the arpeggio and pentatonic approach:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>CAGED Scale Shapes:</strong> Learn each shape ascending and descending from the root note.</li>\n  <li><strong>Harmonized Scales:</strong> Play through all diatonic degrees (I, ii, iii, IV, V, vi) running the scale in each CAGED position.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scales Over Progressions:</strong> Play each chord\'s scale in its CAGED position as the progression moves through changes.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Relative Major/Minor</h3>\n<p>Every major scale has a <strong>relative minor</strong> that uses the same notes but starts from the 6th degree. Example: C major (C D E F G A B) and A minor (A B C D E F G) share all notes but centre on different roots.</p>\n'},"level-4b-pent-pairs":{title:"Pentatonic Pairs: Switching Scales Over Changes",concise:"\n<p><strong>Pentatonic Pairs</strong> is the technique of switching between related pentatonic scales as chords change in a progression. Instead of one scale over everything, you align each chord with its own pentatonic — giving you better chord-tone targeting while staying in the familiar 5-note framework.</p>\n<p><strong>Example in G major:</strong> G major pent over G, A minor pent over Am, B minor pent over Bm, C major pent over C, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Key insight:</strong> For diatonic progressions, each chord's pentatonic is a subset of the parent major scale — so everything stays in key. You're using pentatonic shapes as a <em>lens</em> to focus on each chord's tones.</p>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>The Concept</h3>\n<p>When soloing over chord changes, a single pentatonic scale works but can feel generic. Pentatonic Pairs takes the next step: you assign a matching pentatonic to each chord in the progression and switch between them as the chords change.</p>\n\n<h3>How It Works</h3>\n<p>For a diatonic progression in any major key, each scale degree has an associated pentatonic:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Degree</th><th>Chord</th><th>Pentatonic</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>I</td><td>Major</td><td>Major pentatonic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>ii</td><td>Minor</td><td>Minor pentatonic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>iii</td><td>Minor</td><td>Minor pentatonic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>IV</td><td>Major</td><td>Major pentatonic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>V</td><td>Major</td><td>Major pentatonic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>vi</td><td>Minor</td><td>Minor pentatonic</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Each pentatonic contains (almost) only notes from the parent key scale, so switching between them is harmonically safe.</p>\n\n<h3>Finding Pentatonic Pairs on the Fretboard</h3>\n<p>The beauty of this approach is that you already know the shapes — you're just shifting which root note you centre on. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Position 1 of G major pentatonic overlaps with position 2 of A minor pentatonic</li>\n  <li>The shapes physically overlap on the fretboard</li>\n  <li>You don't need to \"jump\" to a new position — just shift your focus to different root notes within the same area</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Practical Application</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Identify the key and chord progression</li>\n  <li>Map each chord to its pentatonic</li>\n  <li>Practise switching pentatonics on each chord change</li>\n  <li>Focus on landing on chord tones (especially the root and 3rd) when each new chord arrives</li>\n  <li>Use scale tones as connectors between chord tones</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Beyond Diatonic Progressions</h3>\n<p>Pentatonic pairs really shine with non-diatonic chords. If a chord doesn't belong to the key, you can still use that chord's pentatonic — the 5-note structure avoids most clashes and naturally highlights the chord's unique tones.</p>\n\n<h3>Connection to improvisation</h3>\n<p>Pentatonic pairs are a core improvisation technique. They provide a simple, extensible system: start with one pentatonic over everything, then gradually add chord-specific switching as your ear and technique develop.</p>\n"},"level-5b-diatonic-ext":{title:"Major & Minor Scales: The Full Diatonic System",concise:"\n<p>This topic has been merged into <strong>Diatonic Scales</strong>. The exercises below cover the same material: CAGED scale shapes, harmonized scales, and scales over progressions.</p>\n<p>The scale <em>extends</em> the pentatonic by adding two notes: the <strong>4th &amp; 7th</strong> (major) or <strong>2nd &amp; ♭6th</strong> (minor).</p>\n",detailed:"\n<p>See the <strong>Diatonic Scales</strong> theory section for full details on major and minor scale construction, CAGED positions, and exercise structure.</p>\n"},"level-6b-connections":{title:"Connections & Transitions: Moving Freely Across the Fretboard",concise:"\n<p>This chapter teaches four practical techniques for <strong>connecting positions</strong> while improvising, so you stop being trapped in one box:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Octaves</strong> — replay the same lick 12 frets apart, or move diagonally using extended pentatonic patterns</li>\n  <li><strong>Intervallic pairs (6ths)</strong> — remove the middle note of a triad to get 6ths; use them as double-stop transitions between positions</li>\n  <li><strong>Linear movements</strong> — arpeggios, 2-string pentatonic runs, or simple scale ascensions that walk you step-wise into a new position</li>\n  <li><strong>Half-step slides</strong> — slide into the next chord from one fret below for a seamless, musical-sounding transition</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Work each technique in isolation first, then combine them all over backing tracks to improvise freely up and down the entire neck.</p>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Why Connections Matter</h3>\n<p>By now you can improvise within a triad/pentatonic pair, but you may still feel <strong>stuck in one area</strong> of the fretboard. Connections give you concrete methods to move between positions smoothly while keeping your lines musical and grounded in the chord tones.</p>\n<p>There are four main techniques, each suited to different situations.</p>\n\n<h3>1 — Octaves</h3>\n<p>Octaves are the simplest way to shift position. The guitar splits into two octaves (open–fret 12, fret 12+), so you can <strong>play the exact same lick in both octaves</strong> using the same fingering on the same strings — just 12 frets apart.</p>\n<p>More powerfully, you can move <strong>diagonally</strong> across the fretboard using an extended pentatonic pattern. For example, in B minor you can start with the root-position triad on strings 4-5-6 and play a lick, then replay it an octave higher on strings 1-2-3 using the 2nd-inversion triad — all within one extended pentatonic shape. The octave lines don't have to be identical; varying just a note or two spices things up.</p>\n<p><strong>Extended pentatonic patterns</strong> stretch the standard 3-string pentatonic across more strings. They exist for both major and minor pentatonic and are the key to making diagonal octave movements work.</p>\n<h4>Try it</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Pick any minor pentatonic position. Play a 2-bar lick on strings 4-5-6.</li>\n  <li>Find the same notes an octave higher on strings 1-2-3 (two strings up, two frets up — or three frets for the B-string offset).</li>\n  <li>Replay the lick there. Then try changing one or two notes in the repeat.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2 — Intervallic Pairs (6ths)</h3>\n<p>Intervallic pairs are <strong>double stops created by removing the middle note of a triad</strong>. If the bass note is the root, you get a 5th; if the 3rd or 5th is in the bass, you get a 6th. Since you already know your triad shapes on each string group, these pairs are immediately accessible.</p>\n<h4>How to build them</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Take a string group (e.g. strings 1-2-3). Lay out the three triad inversions (root position, 1st inversion, 2nd inversion).</li>\n  <li>Remove the middle note of each triad — the remaining two notes form a 5th or 6th.</li>\n  <li>Play through all three shapes ascending, then descending. You're harmonising the scale in 6ths.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You can also harmonise the scale: play through all the diatonic triads on one string group, remove the middle note of each, and you get a smooth ascending/descending line of 6ths.</p>\n<h4>Example (A major, strings 1-2-3)</h4>\n<p>The three A major triads on strings 1-2-3 give you: 6th (1st inv, 3rd in bass) → 6th (2nd inv, 5th in bass) → 5th (root position, root in bass). Ascending through these intervallic pairs over A major, then landing on the root B on the chord change to B minor, creates a strong, musical transition.</p>\n<p>6ths work great ascending <em>and</em> descending, and you don't need to play every 6th in the scale — pick the ones that sound right.</p>\n\n<h3>3 — Linear Movements</h3>\n<p>Linear movements progress <strong>step-wise up or down the neck</strong> — a gradual transition rather than a big jump.</p>\n<h4>Three common approaches</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Arpeggio-based:</strong> Use an arpeggio to connect two triad positions. For example, an A major arpeggio starting from the major 3rd on the 14th fret (string 2) descending to the root on the 7th fret (string 4) smoothly takes you from one triad position to another.</li>\n  <li><strong>2-string pentatonic runs:</strong> Play pentatonic notes on just two adjacent strings to walk between positions. In B minor, you can use a 2-string pentatonic run to move from the 1st-inversion triad to the root-position triad.</li>\n  <li><strong>Simple scale ascension:</strong> Sometimes all you need is to climb or descend through the scale. For example, ascending from the root-position B minor triad on strings 1-2-3 up to the 1st inversion by simply playing each scale tone in sequence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The key is that the movement sounds <strong>intentional and melodic</strong>, not like an exercise.</p>\n\n<h3>4 — Half-Step Slides</h3>\n<p>Sliding into the next chord from one fret below is one of the most <strong>seamless and musical</strong> transitions available. It works especially well when you want to stay in roughly the same fretboard area rather than making big jumps.</p>\n<h4>How it works</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Identify where you'll land on the next chord (e.g. the root or 5th of the new chord).</li>\n  <li>Find the note one fret below that landing spot.</li>\n  <li>On the chord change, <strong>slide up one fret</strong> into your target note, then continue your phrase.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example (A → C♯m → Bm in the key of A)</h4>\n<p>Transitioning from A major to C♯ minor: slide down from the root of A to the 5th of C♯m (which is also the 7th of A major) — a half-step slide. Then from C♯ minor to B minor: slide up a half step from the root of C♯m to the 5th of Bm. Not every chord change gives you a half-step slide, but <strong>whole-step slides</strong> are just as effective.</p>\n<p>The slide creates a voice-leading effect that makes the transition sound cohesive rather than abrupt.</p>\n\n<h3>Putting It All Together</h3>\n<p>Each technique works in different situations:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Technique</th><th>Best for</th><th>Sound character</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Octaves</td><td>Big position jumps, dramatic effect</td><td>Same melody restated — bold, declarative</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>6ths</td><td>Harmonised movement, double-stop colour</td><td>Sweet, chiming, country/R&B flavour</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Linear</td><td>Gradual position changes within a phrase</td><td>Smooth, flowing, scalar</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Slides</td><td>Chord-change transitions</td><td>Seamless, jazz/blues voice-leading</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>In a real solo you'll <strong>mix all four freely</strong>. A typical pass might use a slide into the IV chord, an octave jump to change register, a linear pentatonic run back down, and 6ths to add texture. The goal is for your position changes to become <strong>musical choices</strong>, not physical obstacles.</p>\n\n<h3>Musical Expression</h3>\n<p>Once the mechanics are comfortable, focus on <strong>how</strong> you play, not just what:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Phrasing:</strong> Create musical sentences — a beginning, a peak, a resolution. Leave space between phrases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamics:</strong> Vary your attack. A soft phrase followed by an aggressive bend tells a story.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rhythmic variety:</strong> Mix 8th notes, triplets, held notes, and rests. Rhythm is at least as important as note choice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Call and response:</strong> Play a short motif, then \"answer\" it — maybe in a different octave or with one of the connection techniques.</li>\n  <li><strong>Motif development:</strong> Take a short 3-4 note idea and evolve it over several bars — transpose it, stretch it rhythmically, vary the ending.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tension and release:</strong> Build intensity (higher notes, faster rhythm, dissonance) then resolve (land on a chord tone, slow down, drop to a lower register).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Continuing Growth</h3>\n<p>The improvisation framework — triads, pentatonic/diatonic pairing, and these connection techniques — gives you the scaffolding to improvise anywhere on the fretboard. From here you can expand into:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Modes</strong> (Dorian, Mixolydian, Lydian) for different colour over the same changes</li>\n  <li><strong>Extended chords</strong> (9ths, 11ths, 13ths) for richer chord-tone targets</li>\n  <li><strong>Chromatic approach notes</strong> for bebop and jazz flavour</li>\n  <li><strong>Genre application:</strong> the framework adapts to blues, country, jazz, fusion — the connection techniques are universal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Continue applying these techniques outside the exercises — to songs you enjoy, jam sessions, and new keys. The more you use them in real musical contexts, the more invisible the framework becomes.</p>\n"},"ear-note-identification":{title:"Ear Training: Note Identification",concise:"\n<p><strong>Note identification</strong> is the ability to recognise which pitch is being played. This is a foundational ear training skill that directly connects to your fretboard knowledge.</p>\n<p>When you can identify notes by ear, you can figure out melodies, find your place during a jam, and tune your guitar without a tuner.</p>\n<h4>How to practice</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Start with reference pitches</strong> — learn what A (440 Hz) sounds like. Use it as an anchor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use open strings</strong> — E, A, D, G, B are great reference points you hear every time you tune.</li>\n  <li><strong>Narrow the field</strong> — start with just natural notes (no sharps/flats), then expand.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fretboard tap mode</strong> — trains your ear <em>and</em> fretboard note knowledge simultaneously.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Answer modes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Buttons</strong> — pick the note name from choices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fretboard tap</strong> — tap the note directly on the fretboard (tests ear + fretboard knowledge).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Shortcuts:</em> Space = replay, 1–9 = pick button, Enter/→ = next, Esc = back.</p>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Why Note Identification Matters</h3>\n<p>Knowing the 12 notes by ear is the audio counterpart of knowing them on the fretboard. Together, they give you complete command: you can <em>see</em> a note and know its sound, or <em>hear</em> a note and know where to find it.</p>\n<p>This skill also underpins all other ear training — intervals, chords, and progressions are all built from individual notes.</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Strategy</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Phase 1 — Natural notes only:</strong> Start with C, D, E, F, G, A, B. These 7 notes are the white keys and form the major scale. Get comfortable identifying them before adding sharps/flats.</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 2 — Add sharps/flats:</strong> Once natural notes feel solid, introduce the remaining 5 notes one at a time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 3 — Fretboard tap:</strong> Switch to fretboard tap mode to combine ear recognition with fretboard position knowledge. This dual skill is extremely powerful.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Connection to Fretboard</h3>\n<p>This drill pairs directly with the <strong>Fretboard Notes</strong> level in Path. Practice both together: learn notes visually on the fretboard, then test whether you can recognise them by sound alone.</p>\n"},"ear-interval-identification":{title:"Ear Training: Interval Recognition",concise:"\n<p><strong>Intervals</strong> — the distance between two pitches — are the cornerstone of understanding the guitar fretboard. If notes are the alphabet, intervals are the words.</p>\n<p>Recognising intervals by ear lets you learn songs faster, improvise confidently, and communicate with other musicians.</p>\n<h4>How to practice</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Start big</strong> — learn Perfect 5th (P5) and Octave (P8) first. They're the most distinctive.</li>\n  <li><strong>Add Perfect 4th</strong> — the P4 is everywhere in guitar tuning (every string pair except G→B).</li>\n  <li><strong>Then thirds</strong> — Major 3rd (bright, happy) vs Minor 3rd (dark, sad). This is the major/minor distinction.</li>\n  <li><strong>Finally seconds and tritone</strong> — M2/m2 are steps, the tritone (TT) is the most dissonant interval.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Answer modes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Buttons</strong> — pick the interval name. Tests <em>ear recognition</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fretboard tap</strong> — tap the exact target note. Tests ear <em>and</em> fretboard knowledge. When the root is hidden (★ off), tap <strong>two notes</strong>: root first, then interval note.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Shortcuts:</em> Space = replay, 1–9 = pick button, Enter/→ = next, Esc = back.</p>\n",detailed:'\n<h3>What Is an Interval?</h3>\n<p>An interval is the distance between two pitches, measured in semitones (half steps). On guitar, 1 semitone = 1 fret.</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Interval</th><th>Semitones</th><th>Sound Character</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 2nd (m2)</td><td>1</td><td>Dissonant, tense — "Jaws" theme</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 2nd (M2)</td><td>2</td><td>Whole step — scale movement</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 3rd (m3)</td><td>3</td><td>Sad, dark — start of a minor chord</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 3rd (M3)</td><td>4</td><td>Bright, happy — start of a major chord</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect 4th (P4)</td><td>5</td><td>Open, strong — "Here Comes the Bride"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tritone (TT)</td><td>6</td><td>Unstable, dissonant — "The Simpsons"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Perfect 5th (P5)</td><td>7</td><td>Strong, powerful — "Star Wars" theme</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 6th (m6)</td><td>8</td><td>Bittersweet — movie love themes</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 6th (M6)</td><td>9</td><td>Warm, familiar — "My Bonnie Lies Over the Ocean"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor 7th (m7)</td><td>10</td><td>Bluesy, unresolved — "Star Trek" theme</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major 7th (M7)</td><td>11</td><td>Dreamlike, jazz — "Take On Me" intro</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Octave (P8)</td><td>12</td><td>Same note, higher — "Somewhere Over the Rainbow"</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Ascending vs Descending vs Harmonic</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ascending:</strong> low note then high note — most natural to learn first.</li>\n  <li><strong>Descending:</strong> high note then low note — intervals sound different going down!</li>\n  <li><strong>Harmonic:</strong> both notes at once — how you\'ll actually hear them in chords.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Start with ascending, then add descending and harmonic as you get comfortable.</p>\n\n<h3>Connection to Fretboard</h3>\n<p>Intervals create the shapes and patterns you see on the guitar. Every chord shape, scale pattern, and arpeggio is defined by its intervals. Training your ear to recognise them makes the fretboard patterns <em>meaningful</em> rather than just memorised shapes.</p>\n<p>This drill pairs with the <strong>Intervals</strong> level in Path — learn the visual shapes on the fretboard, then test your ear.</p>\n'},"ear-interval-comparison":{title:"Ear Training: Interval Comparison",concise:'\n<p><strong>Interval comparison</strong> trains your ability to judge the <em>relative size</em> of intervals — which one is larger?</p>\n<p>This builds a more nuanced sense of pitch distance, helping you gauge musical tension and resolution intuitively.</p>\n<h4>How to practice</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Start with contrasting pairs</strong> — e.g. minor 2nd vs octave. The difference is obvious.</li>\n  <li><strong>Narrow the gap</strong> — compare adjacent intervals like P4 vs P5, or m3 vs M3.</li>\n  <li><strong>Listen for tension</strong> — larger intervals feel more "open", smaller ones more "close" or "tight".</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two intervals are played back-to-back. Pick whether the 1st or 2nd interval is larger using <strong>buttons</strong>.</p>\n',detailed:'\n<h3>Why Comparison Matters</h3>\n<p>Identifying exact intervals is important, but in real musical situations you also need a quick intuition for <em>relative</em> distance. When you hear a melody move, your brain is constantly comparing — "that jump was bigger than the last one".</p>\n<p>This is especially useful for:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Melody transcription:</strong> Quickly gauging whether a note went up a little or a lot.</li>\n  <li><strong>Improvisation:</strong> Feeling the "size" of your musical jumps as you play.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tuning:</strong> Sensing whether an interval is wider or narrower than it should be.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Practice Approach</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Start with pairs that are far apart (m2 vs P8, m3 vs M6).</li>\n  <li>Gradually reduce the gap between the two intervals.</li>\n  <li>The hardest comparisons are adjacent intervals (m3 vs M3, P4 vs TT) — these require the finest ear.</li>\n</ol>\n'},"ear-chord-quality":{title:"Ear Training: Chord Recognition",concise:'\n<p>Recognising chord <strong>quality</strong> means hearing the difference between major, minor, diminished, and augmented chords — plus 7th variations and inversions.</p>\n<p>This is essential for playing by ear, transcribing songs, and knowing where you are in a progression.</p>\n<h4>How to practice</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Major vs Minor first</strong> — this is the most important distinction. Major = bright/happy, Minor = dark/sad.</li>\n  <li><strong>Add diminished &amp; augmented</strong> — diminished = tense/unstable, augmented = mysterious/unresolved.</li>\n  <li><strong>Then 7th chords</strong> — Major 7th (dreamy), Minor 7th (mellow), Dominant 7th (bluesy tension).</li>\n  <li><strong>Inversions</strong> — same chord, different voicing. Root position sounds "grounded", inversions sound "lighter".</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Question modes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chord Quality</strong> — identify the chord type (Major, Minor, Dim, etc.).</li>\n  <li><strong>Inversion</strong> — identify which inversion is playing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chord Tone</strong> — a chord plays, then a single tone is highlighted; name which chord tone (Root, 3rd, 5th, 7th).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Playback:</strong> Block = all notes at once. Arpeggiated = notes one by one.</p>\n<p>Answer via <strong>buttons</strong> or <strong>fretboard tap</strong>. <em>Shortcuts:</em> Space = replay, 1–9 = pick button.</p>\n',detailed:"\n<h3>Chord Qualities Explained</h3>\n<p>A chord's quality comes from its <strong>stacked thirds</strong> — the intervals between its notes:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Quality</th><th>Stacked Thirds</th><th>Sound Character</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Major</td><td>Major 3rd + Minor 3rd (4+3 semitones)</td><td>Bright, happy, resolved</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Minor</td><td>Minor 3rd + Major 3rd (3+4 semitones)</td><td>Dark, sad, introspective</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Diminished</td><td>Minor 3rd + Minor 3rd (3+3 semitones)</td><td>Tense, unstable, needs resolution</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Augmented</td><td>Major 3rd + Major 3rd (4+4 semitones)</td><td>Mysterious, floating, unresolved</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>7th Chords</h3>\n<p>Adding a 7th degree creates richer chords:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major 7th:</strong> Major triad + Major 7th — dreamy, jazzy, sophisticated.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor 7th:</strong> Minor triad + Minor 7th — mellow, smooth, common in jazz/R&B.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dominant 7th:</strong> Major triad + Minor 7th — bluesy tension that wants to resolve.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Block vs Arpeggiated</h3>\n<p>Use <strong>block</strong> (all notes at once) to train chord quality recognition as a whole sound. Use <strong>arpeggiated</strong> (one note at a time) to hear the individual notes — this helps you understand <em>why</em> a chord sounds the way it does.</p>\n\n<h3>Connection to Fretboard</h3>\n<p>This pairs with the <strong>Triads</strong> and <strong>CAGED</strong> levels in Path. Learning the triad shapes visually while training your ear to identify chord qualities creates a powerful bridge between seeing and hearing music.</p>\n"},"ear-chord-progression":{title:"Ear Training: Chord Progressions",concise:'\n<p><strong>Chord progressions</strong> are sequences of chords that form the harmonic backbone of music. Learning to identify them by ear is one of the most practical ear training skills.</p>\n<p>Once you can hear progressions, you can learn songs by ear, follow along in jam sessions, and understand how music moves harmonically.</p>\n<h4>How to practice</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Learn the common progressions</strong> — I-IV-V, I-V-vi-IV, ii-V-I. These cover most popular music.</li>\n  <li><strong>Listen for the "home" chord (I)</strong> — it feels like resolution, like arriving home.</li>\n  <li><strong>Listen for tension chords (V, vii°)</strong> — they create a pull back toward the I chord.</li>\n  <li><strong>Identify the minor chords (ii, iii, vi)</strong> — these add colour and emotional depth.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A chord progression is played; identify the Roman numeral sequence using <strong>buttons</strong>.</p>\n',detailed:'\n<h3>The Roman Numeral System</h3>\n<p>Chords in a key are numbered with Roman numerals based on their position in the scale:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Degree</th><th>Quality</th><th>Function</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>I</td><td>Major</td><td>Home / Tonic — the centre of gravity</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>ii</td><td>Minor</td><td>Subdominant — leads to V</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>iii</td><td>Minor</td><td>Mediant — connects I and V regions</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>IV</td><td>Major</td><td>Subdominant — common movement from I</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>V</td><td>Major</td><td>Dominant — strongest pull back to I</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>vi</td><td>Minor</td><td>Relative minor — sad alternative to I</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>vii°</td><td>Diminished</td><td>Leading tone — tense, resolves to I</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Common Progressions You\'ll Hear Everywhere</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>I-IV-V:</strong> The foundation of rock, blues, and country. Simple, powerful, universal.</li>\n  <li><strong>I-V-vi-IV:</strong> The "pop" progression — countless hit songs use this exact sequence.</li>\n  <li><strong>ii-V-I:</strong> The jazz standard — smooth, sophisticated resolution.</li>\n  <li><strong>I-vi-IV-V:</strong> The "50s" progression — doo-wop, early rock and roll.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Connection to Improvisation</h3>\n<p>Hearing chord progressions is essential for the <strong>Improvisation Framework</strong>. When you can identify the chord changes, you know which triads to target and which pentatonic/diatonic pairs to play over each chord. This is the bridge between ear training and real-time musical performance.</p>\n'},"toolkit-fretboard":{title:"Fretboard Explorer",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Fretboard Explorer</strong> is your interactive reference tool for visualising notes, scales, arpeggios, and chord voicings across the entire fretboard.</p>\n<h4>Three modes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Notes:</strong> See all notes on the fretboard. Tap any note to hear it. Great for memorisation drills — quiz yourself by finding all instances of a specific note.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scales:</strong> Select a root and scale type to see the pattern overlaid on the fretboard. Filter by CAGED position to isolate one shape at a time. Toggle between chord, arpeggio, and full scale views.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chords:</strong> Browse chord voicings for any root. See fingerings, intervals, and hear each voicing.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Using with the Learning Path</h3>\n<p>The Fretboard Explorer is a companion to every level in Path:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Notes level:</strong> Use Notes mode to quiz yourself on fretboard positions. Pick a note and find every instance across all strings.</li>\n  <li><strong>Intervals level:</strong> Switch to Scales mode and observe how interval patterns create shapes. Compare the same interval on different string pairs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Triads level:</strong> Use the chord/arpeggio layer in Scales mode to see triad shapes across string groupings.</li>\n  <li><strong>CAGED level:</strong> Filter by CAGED position to see how each chord form spans the fretboard. Step through C-A-G-E-D positions to see connections.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scales level:</strong> Compare pentatonic and diatonic patterns. See how the pentatonic is a subset of the full scale.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Practice Tips</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>After working through a Path exercise, open the Explorer to <em>see</em> what you just practised.</li>\n  <li>Use it to look up patterns before attempting a new exercise.</li>\n  <li>Compare scale/arpeggio shapes side by side to see how they overlap.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"toolkit-circle-of-fifths":{title:"Circle of Fifths — Deep Dive",concise:'\n<p>The <strong>Circle of Fifths</strong> is a visual map of all 12 keys and their relationships. It shows which keys are closely related, which chords belong to each key, and provides a powerful tool for understanding harmony.</p>\n<h4>What you can do</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Key Info:</strong> Tap any key to see its chords, relative minor/major, key signature, and distance from C.</li>\n  <li><strong>Progressions:</strong> Select common progressions (I-IV-V, I-V-vi-IV, ii-V-I) to see which chords they use in any key.</li>\n  <li><strong>Play:</strong> Hear the scale or chord progression for any key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tap the <strong>"Show Detail"</strong> button below for a deep dive into everything the Circle of Fifths reveals.</p>\n',detailed:"\n<h3>Building the Circle (Sharp Side)</h3>\n<p>Start at <strong>C</strong> (12 o'clock) — no sharps, no flats. Move <strong>clockwise</strong> by a perfect 5th each step. On guitar, a P5 is a <strong>power chord</strong>: same fret on the next thinner string (or 2 frets up across 3→2).</p>\n<p>C → G → D → A → E → B → F♯/G♭</p>\n<p><strong>The rule:</strong> Each clockwise step adds one sharp. The new sharp is always the <strong>7th degree</strong> of the new key's major scale. This is why the sharp side gradually sharpens more notes.</p>\n\n<h3>The Flat Side</h3>\n<p>Now go <strong>counter-clockwise</strong> from C — each step is a perfect 4th up.</p>\n<p>C → F → B♭ → E♭ → A♭ → D♭ → G♭/F♯</p>\n<p><strong>The rule:</strong> Each counter-clockwise step adds one flat. The new flat is always the <strong>4th degree</strong> of the new key's major scale.</p>\n<p>Both sides meet at the bottom (6 o'clock) at the enharmonic key F♯/G♭.</p>\n\n<h3>Every Key on Guitar</h3>\n<p>Each position on the circle is a guitar key with its root note on the fretboard. Moving CW = move up a P5 (power chord shape). Moving CCW = move up a P4 (same fret one string thicker). This is exactly how guitar tuning works — it's tuned in 4ths!</p>\n\n<h3>Enharmonic Notes</h3>\n<p>Three positions on the Circle have two names:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>B / C♭</strong> (5 o'clock)</li>\n  <li><strong>F♯ / G♭</strong> (6 o'clock)</li>\n  <li><strong>C♯ / D♭</strong> (7 o'clock)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Same pitch, different spelling. The choice depends on which key you're in — use the spelling that keeps the scale's letter names in order (A B C D E F G).</p>\n\n<h3>Sharp & Flat Order</h3>\n<p>The <strong>order of sharps</strong> is: <strong>F – C – G – D – A – E – B</strong>. The <strong>order of flats</strong> is the exact reverse: <strong>B – E – A – D – G – C – F</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both sequences are <em>embedded in the circle itself</em>. Read the outer ring from F (11 o'clock) clockwise to B (5 o'clock) = sharp order. Read it backwards = flat order.</p>\n<p><strong>Quick lookup:</strong> Count the steps from C to your key. CW steps = number of sharps (read from F). CCW steps = number of flats (read from B).</p>\n\n<h3>Relative Minor</h3>\n<p>The <strong>inner ring</strong> shows the relative minor of each major key. The rule:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>3 steps clockwise</strong> on the circle (e.g., C major → A minor)</li>\n  <li><strong>3 frets lower</strong> on the same string on guitar</li>\n  <li>The <strong>6th degree</strong> of the major scale = root of the relative minor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Major and its relative minor share <strong>exactly the same key signature</strong> — same sharps/flats. The difference: the minor scale flattens the 3rd, 6th, and 7th compared to the <em>parallel</em> major (same root).</p>\n\n<h3>Chords in a Key</h3>\n<p>This is one of the most powerful Circle insights for songwriters and improvisers:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>I – IV – V</strong> (the 3 primary major chords) are <strong>direct neighbors</strong> on the circle. IV = 1 step CCW, V = 1 step CW.</li>\n  <li>Expand to a <strong>3-segment slice</strong> of the outer ring (root + both neighbors) = your primary chords.</li>\n  <li>Include the <strong>inner ring</strong> below these 3 segments → add ii, iii, vi = all 6 standard chords.</li>\n  <li>Extend one more CW segment for vii° → all <strong>7 diatonic chords</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Songwriting & Analysis</h3>\n<p><strong>Chords that sound good together are neighbors on the Circle.</strong> This means:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If you hear 2–3 chords in a song, find them on the circle — the key is usually the center of that cluster.</li>\n  <li><strong>ii–V–I:</strong> Three neighboring segments played counter-clockwise = descending fifths. The strongest resolution in jazz and pop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Secondary dominants:</strong> The V7 of <em>any</em> chord is one step clockwise from it on the circle. E.g., to tonicize Am, play E7 → Am.</li>\n  <li><strong>Borrowed chords:</strong> Borrow from the <em>parallel minor</em> (same root, minor quality). Especially iv has a strong pull back to I.</li>\n  <li><strong>Circle progressions:</strong> Moving continuously CCW (descending 5ths) creates a powerful forward motion — used in \"I Will Survive\", \"Fly Me to the Moon\", \"Autumn Leaves\".</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Pentatonic Scales</h3>\n<p>The pentatonic scale has a beautiful relationship to the Circle:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Major pentatonic</strong> = <strong>5 consecutive clockwise segments</strong> starting from the root. These are 5 ascending perfect 5ths: R – 5 – 2 – 6 – 3. It's the major scale minus the 4th and 7th.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor pentatonic</strong> = 1 segment CW + 3 segments CCW from root. Notes: R – ♭3 – 4 – 5 – ♭7.</li>\n  <li>All 5 pentatonic notes played together form a warm <strong>6/9 chord</strong> (major) or <strong>m7add11</strong> (minor).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Intervals on the Circle</h3>\n<p>Distance around the circle = musical interval:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>1 step CW = Perfect 5th. 1 step CCW = Perfect 4th.</li>\n  <li>2 CW = Major 2nd. 2 CCW = Minor 7th. And so on.</li>\n  <li><strong>Opposite side</strong> (6 steps) = <strong>Tritone</strong> (augmented 4th / diminished 5th). The most dissonant interval. It occurs between the 4th and 7th degrees of the major scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>\"9 is the magic number\":</strong> Any interval + its inversion = 9. Perfect 5th (5) + Perfect 4th (4) = 9. Major 3rd (3) + Minor 6th (6) = 9. CW and CCW the same number of steps give you complementary intervals.</p>\n\n<h3>Chord Shapes as Geometry</h3>\n<p>Chord types form consistent geometric shapes when plotted on the circle:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Power chord</strong> (R + P5): a <em>line</em> between adjacent segments — the building block of the circle itself.</li>\n  <li><strong>sus2</strong> (R-2-5): 3 consecutive CW segments. Like stacked power chords.</li>\n  <li><strong>sus4</strong> (R-4-5): root in the middle of 3 consecutive segments.</li>\n  <li><strong>Major triad</strong>: a <em>triangle</em> — same shape for all 12 keys.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minor triad</strong>: a <em>mirror-image triangle</em> of the major.</li>\n  <li><strong>Augmented triad</strong>: an <em>equilateral triangle</em> — M3 between each note. Any note can be the root. Only 4 unique augmented chords exist.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diminished 7th</strong>: a <em>square</em> — m3 between each note. Any of the 4 notes can be root. Only 3 unique dim7 chords exist, containing 2 tritones each.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Modes & Brightness</h3>\n<p>The 7 modes have a natural brightness order — and that order IS the Circle itself:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center;\"><strong>Lydian → Ionian → Mixolydian → Dorian → Aeolian → Phrygian → Locrian</strong></p>\n<p>Reading the circle from F (11 o'clock) clockwise to B (5 o'clock) gives exactly this sequence. Each step CCW from Lydian flattens one more note, making the mode progressively darker.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Lydian</strong> (0 CCW steps): brightest — raised 4th (♯4). No tritone below the root.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ionian</strong> (1 CCW): standard major scale. Tritone between 4 and 7.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mixolydian</strong> (2 CCW): major with ♭7. Dominant, bluesy feel.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dorian</strong> (3 CCW): minor with natural 6. Jazzy minor, bittersweet.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aeolian</strong> (4 CCW): natural minor scale. Sad, reflective.</li>\n  <li><strong>Phrygian</strong> (5 CCW): minor with ♭2. Spanish, exotic flavor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Locrian</strong> (6 CCW): darkest — diminished tonic chord. Rarely used as a tonal center.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each mode's unique flavor comes from <strong>where the tritone falls</strong> in the scale. Moving it changes the tension point and the overall color.</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Ideas</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Key walk:</strong> Pick a key, find it on the circle, say its sharps/flats. Move CW, repeat. Cover all 12 keys.</li>\n  <li><strong>Neighbor chords:</strong> Tap a key, note its IV (1 CCW) and V (1 CW). Play I–IV–V on guitar.</li>\n  <li><strong>Relative pairs:</strong> For each major key, find the relative minor 3 steps CW. Play both scales from the same position.</li>\n  <li><strong>Song analysis:</strong> Identify 2–3 chords from a song you know. Find them on the circle — the key is the center.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pentatonic mapping:</strong> Pick a root, highlight 5 CW segments mentally. Those are your major pentatonic notes.</li>\n</ol>\n"},"toolkit-circle-of-thirds":{title:"Circle of Thirds",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Circle of Thirds</strong> arranges the 7 notes of a scale by diatonic thirds instead of fifths. This reveals how triads are built — every triad is simply <em>three consecutive notes</em> on the thirds chain.</p>\n<h4>What you can do</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chain mode:</strong> See the full thirds chain (e.g., C → E → G → B → D → F → A → C) with color-coded intervals — major third (warm) vs minor third (cool).</li>\n  <li><strong>Triads mode:</strong> Step through all 7 diatonic triads in thirds-chain order. Each triad is formed by selecting a node and its two successors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Play:</strong> Hear the thirds chain or individual triads with audio playback.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>How the Circle of Thirds Works</h3>\n<p>A major scale has 7 notes. If you stack them in thirds (skip every other note), you get: <strong>1 → 3 → 5 → 7 → 2 → 4 → 6 → 1</strong>. This chain shows you the alternating pattern of major and minor third intervals that define the key's harmony.</p>\n\n<h3>The Connection to Triads</h3>\n<p>Any three consecutive notes on the thirds chain form a diatonic triad:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1-3-5</strong> → I (major)</li>\n  <li><strong>3-5-7</strong> → iii (minor)</li>\n  <li><strong>5-7-2</strong> → V (major)</li>\n  <li><strong>7-2-4</strong> → vii° (diminished)</li>\n  <li><strong>2-4-6</strong> → ii (minor)</li>\n  <li><strong>4-6-1</strong> → IV (major)</li>\n  <li><strong>6-1-3</strong> → vi (minor)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Triad Harmonization Practice</h3>\n<p>The thirds chain leads directly to three powerful guitar exercises:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Horizontal Leap (one string):</strong> Play up a third, come back a step. This trains your ear to hear diatonic thirds on a single string — e.g., C→E, D→F, E→G, F→A, …</li>\n  <li><strong>Double Stops (two strings):</strong> Play the same thirds as simultaneous pairs on adjacent strings. This develops double-stop technique and harmonic hearing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Positional Triads (three strings):</strong> Play all 7 diatonic triads within a single fretboard position on a string group. This is the foundation for chord melody and voice leading.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Why Thirds Matter</h3>\n<p>Thirds are the <em>building blocks</em> of Western harmony. Understanding the thirds chain gives you:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Instant knowledge of every diatonic chord in any key</li>\n  <li>A roadmap for voice leading between chords</li>\n  <li>The foundation for extended chords (7ths, 9ths — just keep stacking thirds)</li>\n  <li>Better improvisation through chord-tone awareness</li>\n</ul>\n"},"toolkit-metronome":{title:"Metronome",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Metronome</strong> provides a steady tempo reference for your practice. Good timing is one of the most important musical skills — it's what makes the difference between sounding amateur and professional.</p>\n<h4>Practice approach</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Start slow:</strong> Set a comfortable tempo (60–80 BPM) where you can play cleanly with no mistakes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Only increase speed when clean:</strong> If you can play through an exercise 3 times perfectly, bump up by 5 BPM.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use it with Path exercises:</strong> Triads, arpeggios, and scales all benefit from metronome practice.</li>\n</ol>\n",detailed:'\n<h3>Why Timing Matters</h3>\n<p>Even the best note choices sound bad if they\'re not in time. Conversely, simple notes played with great timing sound professional and musical. The metronome is your training partner for developing this sense.</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Strategies</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Subdivision practice:</strong> Play 8th notes or triplets against the click to develop internal rhythm.</li>\n  <li><strong>Accent practice:</strong> Emphasise different beats while keeping the metronome on the downbeat.</li>\n  <li><strong>Slow practice:</strong> Paradoxically, practising slowly is <em>harder</em> than fast. Set it to 40–50 BPM and play scales — this reveals timing weaknesses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tap tempo:</strong> Hear a song you want to learn? Tap along to find the BPM, then set the metronome to that tempo for practice.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The Speed-Up Protocol</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li>Find your "clean tempo" — the speed where you make zero mistakes.</li>\n  <li>Play the exercise 3–5 times perfectly at that tempo.</li>\n  <li>Increase by 5 BPM. Repeat.</li>\n  <li>If you start making mistakes, go back down by 5 BPM. No shortcuts!</li>\n</ol>\n'},"toolkit-drum-machine":{title:"Drum Machine",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Drum Machine</strong> gives you rhythmic context that's more musical than a metronome click. Playing over drum patterns develops your sense of groove and prepares you for playing with other musicians.</p>\n<h4>How to use</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>For improvisation practice:</strong> Set a groove pattern and improvise using triad/pentatonic pairs from the Path.</li>\n  <li><strong>For timing practice:</strong> Use different time signatures and patterns to challenge your rhythmic flexibility.</li>\n  <li><strong>Create patterns:</strong> Build custom drum patterns and export them as backing tracks.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Why Drums Help Your Guitar Playing</h3>\n<p>A metronome gives you a click. Drums give you a <em>groove</em>. The hi-hat, snare, and kick create a rhythmic framework that teaches your body to feel musical time, not just count it.</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Ideas</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chord progression practice:</strong> Set a simple rock beat and practise chord changes from Path. The groove makes it feel like real music.</li>\n  <li><strong>Improvisation:</strong> Create a groove, pick a key, and improvise using the triad/pentatonic framework. This is the closest thing to jamming with a band.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rhythmic exploration:</strong> Try different patterns (straight 8ths, shuffle, funky 16ths) to discover how the same notes feel different over different grooves.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"toolkit-tuner":{title:"Tuner",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Tuner</strong> provides chromatic pitch detection so you can keep your guitar in tune. Accurate tuning is essential — even a slightly out-of-tune guitar makes everything sound wrong.</p>\n<h4>Quick tips</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>Tune in a quiet environment for best accuracy.</li>\n  <li>Pluck the string firmly and let it ring — the tuner needs a clear signal.</li>\n  <li>Tap a string button to hear the reference pitch — useful for training your ear to recognise when you're in tune.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>How Chromatic Tuning Works</h3>\n<p>The tuner detects the frequency of the note you're playing and compares it to the nearest known pitch. The gauge shows whether you're sharp (too high) or flat (too low).</p>\n\n<h3>Standard Tuning Reference</h3>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>String</th><th>Note</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>6 (thickest)</td><td>E2</td><td>82.41 Hz</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>5</td><td>A2</td><td>110.00 Hz</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>4</td><td>D3</td><td>146.83 Hz</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>3</td><td>G3</td><td>196.00 Hz</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>2</td><td>B3</td><td>246.94 Hz</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1 (thinnest)</td><td>E4</td><td>329.63 Hz</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Ear Training Connection</h3>\n<p>As you use the tuner regularly, you'll naturally develop a sense for when your guitar is in tune — a form of ear training that happens automatically.</p>\n"},"toolkit-practice-timer":{title:"Practice Timer",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Practice Timer</strong> helps you track and structure your practice sessions. Consistent, focused practice is far more effective than long, aimless sessions.</p>\n<h4>Suggested structure</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Warm-up (5 min):</strong> Chromatic exercises or easy scales to loosen up fingers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ear Training (5 min):</strong> Quick drill from the Ear tab — keep cumulative skills sharp.</li>\n  <li><strong>Path work (15–20 min):</strong> Work on your current level in the Learning Path.</li>\n  <li><strong>Improvisation / playing (10 min):</strong> Apply what you've learned over backing tracks or songs.</li>\n</ol>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>How to Use the Timer Effectively</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Lap feature:</strong> Use laps to mark transitions between practice sections. This creates a log you can review.</li>\n  <li><strong>Idle detection:</strong> The timer auto-pauses if you stop playing, keeping your session time accurate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Session history:</strong> Review past sessions to track your consistency. Regular short sessions (20–30 min) beat occasional long ones.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The Power of Consistency</h3>\n<p>Research on skill acquisition consistently shows that <strong>distributed practice</strong> (short, frequent sessions) produces better long-term retention than <strong>massed practice</strong> (long, infrequent sessions). Aim for 20–30 minutes daily rather than 2 hours once a week.</p>\n\n<h3>Practice Planning</h3>\n<p>For maximum progress, balance three types of practice:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Technical work:</strong> Exercises from Path — building skills systematically.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ear training:</strong> Drills from the Ear tab — connecting sound to knowledge.</li>\n  <li><strong>Application:</strong> Playing music, improvising, learning songs — making it all meaningful.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"global-learning-guide":{title:"How to Learn Guitar with FretPath",concise:"\n<p><strong>FretPath</strong> is built around a proven approach to guitar learning: start with the smallest building blocks and expand outward, always connecting theory to sound and the fretboard.</p>\n\n<h4>The Four Sections</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>🗺️ Path</strong> — your structured curriculum. Follow it sequentially — each level builds on the previous. Covers notes → intervals → triads → CAGED → arpeggios → pentatonic → diatonic → improvisation.</li>\n  <li><strong>👂 Ear</strong> — train your ear alongside Path. The ability to <em>hear</em> what you learn visually is what transforms pattern memorisation into real musicianship.</li>\n  <li><strong>🧰 Toolkit</strong> — reference and practice tools. Fretboard explorer for looking up patterns, metronome for timing, drum machine for groove, tuner, and practice timer.</li>\n  <li><strong>🎼 Library</strong> — your personal phrase collection. Save licks, riffs, and progressions you discover as you learn.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Quick Start</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Open <strong>Path</strong> and start with <em>Fretboard Notes</em>.</li>\n  <li>After each Path session, spend 5 minutes in <strong>Ear</strong> training the matching skill.</li>\n  <li>Use <strong>Toolkit</strong> tools as references alongside your practice.</li>\n  <li>Save interesting patterns to <strong>Library</strong> as you discover them.</li>\n</ol>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>The Learning Journey</h3>\n<p>FretPath follows the approach of foundational fretboard navigation: dissect the fretboard a piece at a time, starting with the smallest structures and building outward. Each layer reveals relationships between the concepts and shows how interconnected all musical components are.</p>\n\n<h3>Stage 1: Foundation (Path Levels 1–3)</h3>\n<p>Build the essential knowledge that everything else depends on.</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Path Level</th><th>What You Learn</th><th>Ear Training Pairing</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Fretboard Notes</td><td>All 12 notes across 6 strings, octave shapes</td><td>Note Identification drill</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Intervals</td><td>Distance between pitches, fretboard interval shapes</td><td>Interval Recognition + Interval Comparison</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Triads</td><td>3-note chords on all string groups, inversions</td><td>Chord Recognition (Major/Minor)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Practice tip:</strong> Spend 5 minutes on Ear Training at the start of every session. Match it to your current Path level.</p>\n\n<h3>Stage 2: Structure (Path Levels 4–7)</h3>\n<p>Expand your fretboard vision with the CAGED system and scales.</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Path Level</th><th>What You Learn</th><th>Toolkit Pairing</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>CAGED System</td><td>5 movable chord shapes connected up the neck</td><td>Fretboard Explorer — filter by CAGED position</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Arpeggios</td><td>Chord tones played individually, 5 CAGED patterns</td><td>Fretboard Explorer — arpeggio layer</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Pentatonic Scales</td><td>5-note scales in 5 positions, major/minor relationship</td><td>Circle of 5ths — relative major/minor keys</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Diatonic Scales</td><td>Full 7-note major/minor scales</td><td>Metronome — scale practice with tempo</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Practice tip:</strong> Always use a metronome for scale and arpeggio practice. Start slow, increase only when clean.</p>\n\n<h3>Stage 3: Application (Path Levels 8–9)</h3>\n<p>Put it all together with the improvisation framework and musical connections.</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Path Level</th><th>What You Learn</th><th>How to Practice</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Triad/Pentatonic Pairs</td><td>The improvisation framework — triads anchoring pentatonic lines</td><td>Drum Machine for groove-based improv practice</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Connections &amp; Transitions</td><td>Octaves, 6ths, slides, linear runs between positions</td><td>Ear Progressions drill — follow chord changes by ear</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Practice tip:</strong> Record yourself improvising. Playback reveals timing issues and helps you hear your progress over time.</p>\n\n<h3>The Key Principle</h3>\n<p>Everything is connected. Triads are inside chords. Chords are inside arpeggios. Arpeggios are inside scales. The CAGED system organises them all. Understanding these relationships — both visually on the fretboard and aurally through ear training — is what transforms you from someone who plays memorised patterns into a musician who truly understands the instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>Daily Practice Template</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Ear warm-up (5 min):</strong> One or two drills from the Ear tab matching your current level.</li>\n  <li><strong>Path work (15–20 min):</strong> Current level exercises. Use the metronome.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reference check (2 min):</strong> Open the Fretboard Explorer to visualise what you just practised.</li>\n  <li><strong>Free play (10 min):</strong> Improvise, play songs, or experiment. This is where learning becomes music.</li>\n  <li><strong>Save discoveries:</strong> Found a cool lick or pattern? Save it to the Library before you forget!</li>\n</ol>\n"},"toolkit-pattern-canvas":{title:"Pattern Canvas",concise:"\n<p>The <strong>Pattern Canvas</strong> lets you build, save and compare your own fretboard patterns — scale shapes, chord tones, arpeggios, or any mental model you want to internalise.</p>\n\n<h4>Two modes</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>🎨 Paint:</strong> Tap any fret on the fretboard to add a note. Pick a colour brush (Root, 3rd, 5th, 7th, Other) to categorise each note. Tap the same note again to remove it, or tap with a different brush to recolour it.</li>\n  <li><strong>🔀 Compare:</strong> Pick two patterns (your own or any built-in scale) and overlay them on one fretboard. Notes unique to Pattern A keep their colour, notes unique to Pattern B appear as ghosts, and shared tones are highlighted so you can see exactly where two shapes overlap.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Quick start</h4>\n<ol>\n  <li>Choose a <strong>root note</strong> at the top.</li>\n  <li>Select a brush colour and tap frets to paint your shape.</li>\n  <li>Give the pattern a name and hit <strong>💾 Save</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Use <strong>📂 Template</strong> to load any built-in scale as a starting point.</li>\n  <li>Switch to <strong>Compare</strong> to see how two patterns relate.</li>\n</ol>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Working with patterns</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Duplicate</strong> a saved pattern to create variations without losing the original.</li>\n  <li>Changing the <strong>root</strong> in Compare mode automatically transposes custom patterns so you can compare shapes in any key.</li>\n  <li>Built-in formulas include all major/minor scales, modes, pentatonics, blues and more.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Ideas for practice</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Map a CAGED shape:</strong> Paint each chord-tone colour in a single position, save it, then do the next shape.</li>\n  <li><strong>Find common tones:</strong> Compare two adjacent shapes to see shared notes — great for connecting positions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Arpeggio overlay:</strong> Paint a triad, then compare it with the full scale to see which scale tones surround each chord tone.</li>\n  <li><strong>Build your own system:</strong> Create any pattern that makes sense to <em>you</em> — there are no rules!</li>\n</ol>\n"},"cof-drill":{title:"Circle of Fifths Drill",concise:"\n<p>Test your knowledge of the <strong>Circle of Fifths</strong> with three drill modes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Identify:</strong> Tap the correct key on the circle when prompted.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sequence:</strong> Name the next key moving clockwise around the circle.</li>\n  <li><strong>Relative:</strong> Identify the relative minor for each major key.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Why practice this?</h3>\n<p>Internalising the circle of fifths unlocks faster key changes, transposition, and chord substitution. These drills build the reflex so you don't have to think about it on stage.</p>\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Start with <strong>Identify</strong> — get comfortable locating every key on the circle.</li>\n  <li>Move to <strong>Sequence</strong> to build the clockwise/counter-clockwise reflex.</li>\n  <li>Use <strong>Relative</strong> to connect major and minor keys instantly.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"toolkit-tab-editor":{title:"Tab Editor",concise:"\n<p>A full-featured tablature <strong>viewer and editor</strong> powered by alphaTab.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Player mode:</strong> Open any saved phrase or GP file for playback with synchronized cursor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Edit mode:</strong> Toggle editing to input notes, change durations, add bends, slides, and more.</li>\n  <li><strong>Multi-track:</strong> View and mix multiple tracks with the track panel.</li>\n</ul>\n",detailed:"\n<h3>Keyboard shortcuts</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>0-9:</strong> Enter fret numbers on the current string.</li>\n  <li><strong>↑/↓:</strong> Move between strings.</li>\n  <li><strong>←/→:</strong> Navigate beats.</li>\n  <li><strong>Space:</strong> Play / pause.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y:</strong> Undo / redo.</li>\n  <li><strong>?:</strong> Show full shortcut reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Use <strong>New Score</strong> to start a blank tab from scratch.</li>\n  <li>Toggle <strong>notation</strong> view to see standard notation alongside tab.</li>\n  <li>Enable <strong>auto-save</strong> so your edits are never lost.</li>\n</ul>\n"}};